var e="# Privacy & Data Flow\n\n> **Customer-facing documents (2026-10-22):** the Privacy Policy (`/privacy`),\n> the DPA (`/legal/dpa`), the public sub-processor list (`/subprocessors`) and\n> the Trust Center (`/trust`, `/trust-center`). They render from\n> `src/pages/trust/subprocessors.ts`, `src/pages/legal/privacyFacts.ts` and\n> `src/pages/legal/trustCenterData.ts`; the PDFs are rebuilt with\n> `npm run trust:pdfs`. This file is the engineering long form.\n\n> The honest, verifiable answer to _\"where does my data go?\"_ — written for a\n> customer's security team. Everything here is asserted by automated tests\n> (`e2e/privacy.spec.ts`, `e2e/csp.spec.ts`) so it can't quietly drift.\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThe Truehound **demo** (the GitHub Pages build, and any build without a configured\nbackend) is a **100% in-browser application**. It makes **zero** third-party\nnetwork requests and **zero** requests carrying your data. The only network\ntraffic is your browser fetching Truehound's own static assets (HTML, JS, CSS,\nfonts, the DuckDB WebAssembly engine) from the origin that served the page.\n\nWhen you use **Bring-Your-Own-Data** (Settings → Demo Controls → _Import your\ndata_) to upload a CSV/JSON of your service catalog, that data is parsed in\nthe browser, stored in this browser's `localStorage`, and used to regenerate\nthe demo locally. **It is never transmitted anywhere.** This is the literal\nmeaning of the on-screen promise _\"Your data never leaves this browser.\"_\n\nOne deliberate exception, on the hosted site only: the \"Try the live demo\"\nform sends `{ first, last, email, utm, page }` to our cloud connector so we\ncan follow up, when (and only when) the build has a connector and that\nconnector advertises lead capture. See _Live-demo leads and the visitor's\nname_ below.\n\n## What crosses the network (demo build)\n\n| Request                                      | Destination                                     | Carries your data? |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| HTML / JS / CSS chunks                       | The serving origin (same-origin)                | No                 |\n| Web fonts (Figtree, JetBrains Mono `.woff2`) | The serving origin (`/fonts/`, **self-hosted**) | No                 |\n| DuckDB-WASM engine + worker (`.wasm`, `.js`) | The serving origin (same-origin)                | No                 |\n| Favicons / manifest / icons                  | The serving origin                              | No                 |\n\nThat's the complete list. There is:\n\n- **No** analytics/telemetry beacon. The analytics and structured-logger\n  services buffer to `localStorage` only (`src/services/analytics.ts`,\n  `src/services/logger.ts`); they have no network transport in the demo.\n  (A hosted deploy can opt in by setting `VITE_POSTHOG_KEY` at build time —\n  see _Hosted-deploy product telemetry_ below. The demo build leaves it\n  unset, so the loader injects nothing.)\n- **No** error-reporting SDK (no Sentry/Datadog/etc.).\n- **No** third-party font CDN. The Figtree / JetBrains Mono faces are served\n  from `public/fonts/` — the former `fonts.googleapis.com` / `fonts.gstatic.com`\n  requests (which leak the visitor's IP + User-Agent to Google on first paint)\n  were removed. See _Self-hosted fonts_ below.\n- **No** DuckDB extension CDN. The `/catalog` page hydrates an in-browser\n  DuckDB table using **core SQL only** (`src/lib/duckdb/hydrate.ts`). It does\n  **not** use `read_json_auto`, which would lazily fetch the `json` extension\n  from `https://extensions.duckdb.org` — a third-party request that previously\n  fired the moment you opened `/catalog`.\n\nThe Content-Security-Policy (`scripts/csp-plugin.mjs`) enforces this at the\nbrowser level: `default-src 'self'`, no remote origins in `style-src`,\n`font-src`, `img-src`, or `connect-src`. A request to any off-origin host is\nblocked by the browser, not merely \"not made\". See\n[`docs/SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md) → _Transport & browser security_.\n\n### When a backend is configured (production)\n\nSetting `VITE_BFF_URL` at build time activates the HTTP repository, and Truehound\nthen talks to **that one BFF origin** (and only that origin — the CSP appends\nit to `connect-src` automatically). Your data flows to your own BFF, which you\noperate. No other destinations are added. Telemetry/preferences/saved-views\nPOSTs are all gated on `VITE_BFF_URL` being set and target only that origin\n(`src/data/mcp/telemetry.ts`, `src/hooks/usePreferences.ts`,\n`src/hooks/useSavedViews.ts`). The demo build leaves `VITE_BFF_URL` unset, so\nnone of those code paths can reach the network.\n\n### When the AI layer is enabled (opt-in, connector with an Anthropic key)\n\nOff unless a workspace admin adds the workspace's own Anthropic key in Settings\n> AI ([AI_LAYER.md](AI_LAYER.md)); the key is stored encrypted and the calls are\nbilled by Anthropic to that account. The SPA shows AI controls only when the\nworkspace has a key. Even then, nothing is sent\nuntil a user clicks an AI button. On that click the browser posts **repo facts\nit already read** (CODEOWNERS / catalog-info.yaml / governance-file text,\ncommit author logins with counts, a linked PagerDuty service and team name) to\nthe connector, which forwards them to Anthropic's API (Claude) and returns a\nvalidated answer. **No token or vendor key is ever part of that request**: the\nGitHub token stays in this browser and is used only for direct calls to\n`api.github.com`, including opening a fix pull request when the user clicks\n\"Open a pull request\" (the one exception is the Access Audit's GitHub search\nrelay, described under _Access Audit_ below). The connector stores nothing.\n\n### When a real account connects GitHub without a token\n\nThe Truth Scan reads CODEOWNERS and `catalog-info.yaml` bytes from\n`raw.githubusercontent.com` (GitHub's own raw-content host, which does not\ncount against the 60-an-hour anonymous API budget) and tree listings from\n`api.github.com`. Both are plain GETs for PUBLIC repo files; no token, cookie\nor credential is ever sent to the raw host. With a token, every read goes to\n`api.github.com` only. The CSP `connect-src` lists exactly these origins.\nWhen Autopilot is on (paid plan) and the browser-held token can push, a safe\nfix is opened as a pull request on `api.github.com`; without one, nothing is\nwritten and the change is offered as a draft link to GitHub's editor.\n\n## What is stored in your browser\n\nTruehound persists UI state and (if you use it) your imported data to\n`localStorage`. Nothing is stored in cookies by the demo. The keys fall into\nthese families:\n\n| Key family                     | Example                                                                       | Contents                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Sensitivity                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Imported data (BYOD)**       | `atlas.byod.import.v1`                                                        | Your uploaded service/team/person names                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Holds data you provided. Stays local.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Connected workspace**        | `atlas.live.workspace.v1`                                                     | The snapshot pulled from sources YOU connected (services, incidents, on-call, SLOs, monitors)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | **Your operational data.** Stays local; cleared on disconnect. On sign-out it moves to the parked-workspace key below (it no longer sits in the live key).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Parked workspace**           | `atlas:parked-workspaces.v1`                                                  | A signed-out real account's connected workspace + score history + its proof chain (`atlas.actions.v1`, `atlas.actions-audit.v1`), Autopilot policy (`atlas.autopilot-policy.v1`) and finding ledger (`atlas.findings-ledger.v1`), keyed by its email (at most 3). **Never its access tokens.**                                                                                                                                               | **Your operational data.** Stays local. Restored only when the same email signs back in (\"Welcome back\" on the sign-in page, or Get started with that email). \"Remove my workspace from this browser\" removes its data, both on the sign-in card and on the sign-out confirmation; a paid plan stays parked so the customer is not locked out (\"Forget this account on this browser\" removes that too).                                                                                                             |\n| **Git-host tokens**            | `atlas.live.token.github.v1` (gitlab/bitbucket likewise)                      | The access token you pasted. **GitHub (added 2026-10-21): kept for this browser session only by default** (`sessionStorage`, or memory when that is unavailable), so closing the browser forgets it. Only when you tick \"Remember this token on this device\" on the connect card is it kept in `localStorage`, so re-sync and Always watching's re-scan on visit work without re-pasting. GitLab/Bitbucket tokens are kept in `localStorage` | **Highest sensitivity: plaintext in browser storage.** A token with repo write access can change GitHub. Use a least-privilege token (or, to let this browser open fix pull requests, one scoped to `contents` + `pull requests` write on the repos you choose). \"Forget token\" on the Access Audit page removes it from both places at once; also removed on disconnect and sign-out (sign-out clears the session copy too). PagerDuty/Datadog keys are NEVER persisted: they live only in memory during the pull. |\n| **Catalog imports**            | `atlas.live.imports.v1`                                                       | Fields merged from a Backstage, Port, Cortex or OpsLevel export you imported (owner, tier, description per matched repo), each batch tagged with its source file and time                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Your catalog data.** Stays local; the export file is read in the browser and never uploaded. Each import is undoable on its own. Parked with the workspace on sign-out.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Weekly digest subscription** | `atlas.live.digest.v1`                                                        | Whether you opted in to the weekly Slack digest, your Slack incoming-webhook URL, the week last posted, and the finding keys already reported                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | **Sensitive: the webhook URL lets anyone holding it post to that channel.** Plaintext in localStorage, like the git tokens. Sent only to the connector relay for each post, which forwards it to Slack and stores nothing. Removed by Unsubscribe and on sign-out.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| Auth session                   | `atlas.session.v1`                                                            | Demo username + role + expiry (no password)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Low — demo identity only                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| Theme / device prefs           | `atlas.theme`, `atlas.prefs.*`                                                | Light/dark, density, landing page                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | None                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Onboarding / tour flags        | `atlas:welcome-completed`, `atlas:tour-completed`, `atlas.onboarding.seen.v3` | \"already seen\" booleans                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | None                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Navigation memory              | `atlas.nav.recentRoutes`, `atlas.nav.bookmarks`, `atlas:cmdk:recent-*`        | Routes you visited, pinned pages                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Low                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| List/table view state          | `atlas.saved-views.*`, `atlas:columns:*`, `atlas:density:*`                   | Column layout, filters, saved queries                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Low                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Snooze / dismissal state       | `atlas:*-snooze`, `atlas.insights.dismissed`, `atlas:highlights:acknowledged` | Which cards/alerts you dismissed                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Low                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Buffered analytics / logs      | `atlas.analytics.queue.v1`, `atlas.errorLog.v1`, `atlas.telemetry.*`          | Local event buffer (never uploaded in the demo)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Low                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Catalog / DuckDB scratch       | `atlas:catalog:query-history`, `atlas:catalog:pinned-queries`                 | SQL you typed on `/catalog`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Low (could echo imported names)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n\n### Live-demo leads and the visitor's name\n\n\"Try the live demo\" asks for a first name, last name and work email, then\nopens the demo. The small print says it plainly: \"We'll email you about\nTruehound. Unsubscribe anytime.\"\n\n- `atlas.demo.visitor.v1` holds `{ first, last }`, in this browser only, so the\n  demo greets the visitor by name (greeting, avatar initials, profile menu,\n  \"you\" labels). It changes the demo persona's display name only, never its\n  identity. Settings > \"Reset demo data\" removes it.\n- The lead `{ first, last, email, marketingConsent, utm, page }` (`utm` = any\n  `utm_*` params on the link, `page` = the route the form was opened from;\n  `marketingConsent` is `true` only when the visitor ticked the unticked-by-\n  default \"Email me about Truehound (optional)\" box, and nobody is emailed\n  about Truehound without it) is sent to the cloud\n  connector's `POST /lead` **only** when this build has a connector\n  (`VITE_CONNECTOR_URL`) **and** its `/health` lists the `leads` feature (a\n  deploy with the `LEADS` KV namespace bound). The connector stores exactly\n  those fields plus a server timestamp `ts`, nothing else: no IP address, no\n  user agent, no cookies. The client IP is used in memory for rate limiting\n  only and is never written.\n- Otherwise (no connector, an older connector, an error or a slow answer) the\n  lead stays in this browser only, under `atlas.demo.leads.v1` (the last five),\n  and is never sent. The demo opens either way.\n- Leads are read back only by the operator with `scripts/list-leads.mjs`\n  (bearer `LEADS_ADMIN_TOKEN`) as CSV. A visitor who wants their lead deleted\n  emails the sales address; the operator deletes the KV key.\n- Unsubscribe: `#/unsubscribe?e=<email>` (linked from every non-transactional\n  email, see `docs/launch/EMAIL_TEMPLATES.md`) calls the connector's\n  `POST /lead/unsubscribe`, which stores `unsub:<email>` = `{ email, ts }` in\n  the same KV namespace (idempotent; works whether or not a lead exists).\n  `scripts/list-leads.mjs` leaves unsubscribed addresses out by default.\n  Replies saying STOP are honoured by hand. Every request is honoured within\n  10 business days.\n- Analytics events (`demo_lead.opened`, `demo_lead.submitted`, ...) carry no\n  name or email, only the outcome.\n\n### Free scan \"Email me this report\"\n\nThe free scan (`#/scan`) runs in the browser and stores nothing on our side.\nUnder the result, an optional form offers to email the summary:\n\n- Nothing is sent unless the visitor types an email **and** ticks the\n  unticked-by-default consent box (\"Email me this scan's summary. I agree that\n  Truehound stores my email with this org name, score and finding count to send\n  it\"). A second, also unticked box (\"Also email me now and then about\n  Truehound\") sets `marketingConsent`.\n- The request is `{ kind: \"scan-report\", email, marketingConsent, report: { org,\nscore, findings }, utm, page }`. It is POSTed to the connector's `POST /lead`\n  only when this build has a connector **and** its `/health` lists `leads`; the\n  connector validates it (`connector/leads.mjs`, names optional for this kind)\n  and stores exactly those fields plus `ts`. Never names, repos, findings\n  detail or the GitHub token.\n- Otherwise the form says, before and after submitting, that the request is\n  kept in this browser only (`atlas.scan.reportRequests.v1`, the last five) and\n  was not sent, so no email will arrive. A \"Download the summary (.txt)\" button\n  gives the same summary as a file either way.\n- `scripts/list-leads.mjs` exports these with `kind`, `report_org`,\n  `report_score` and `report_findings` columns. Unsubscribe works as for demo\n  leads.\n\n### Announcement bar and cookies\n\nThe free-scan announcement bar on the public pages can be dismissed; the\nchoice is kept in this browser only, under `atlas.announce.freeScan.v1`\n(`\"dismissed\"`). No cookie is set for it. The site sets no cookies at all\nwhile analytics is off (no `VITE_POSTHOG_KEY`); the \"No tracking cookies\"\nline on the resource pages is shown only in that case, and disappears by\nitself if analytics is switched on.\n\n### Terms acceptance\n\nThe signup asks the person to tick \"I agree to the Terms of Service and\nPrivacy Policy\" before the plan step. The acceptance is recorded in this\nbrowser only, under `atlas:terms-acceptance.v1`, as `{ termsVersion,\nacceptedAt, email }` (`src/lib/termsConsent.ts`). It is colon-namespaced so\nsign-out keeps it. Settings > Privacy & Telemetry > Legal shows it. The\npublic pages are `#/terms` and `#/privacy`; the policy there is the short,\ncustomer-facing form of this document.\n\n### Payment and entitlement (paid before use)\n\nA real workspace opens only once it is paid. Whether it is paid is checked\n**through the cloud connector**, and the answer is then **cached in this\nbrowser**:\n\n- `atlas.workspace.entitlement.v1` holds `{ status, plan, email, via,\nsessionId?, verifiedAt? }`: the plan picked at signup (`pending`), or the\n  connector's confirmation (`paid`, via a Stripe Checkout Session or a sales\n  activation code). A `pending` record can also carry the `sessionId` of a\n  Stripe return the connector could not confirm yet, so the pay screen says\n  \"do not pay again\" and keeps re-checking it. It is parked with the\n  workspace on sign-out, so a paying customer who signs back in with the same\n  email is not locked out again; \"Remove my workspace from this browser\"\n  removes the workspace data but keeps this paid record parked (the sign-in\n  card's \"Forget this account on this browser\" removes it too).\n- The Stripe return page also works signed out (Stripe can land the\n  customer in a browser with no account): a verified payment is cached the\n  same way, and the account created next in this browser opens paid.\n- To confirm a Stripe payment the browser sends the connector the Checkout\n  Session id from Stripe's redirect (`GET /billing/verify?session_id=`); the\n  connector asks Stripe with the operator's secret key and returns only\n  `{ paid, plan, customerEmail }`. To use an activation code the browser sends\n  the code and the account email (`POST /billing/activate`). Nothing else is\n  sent, and the connector stores nothing.\n- Card details never touch this app or the connector: they are entered on\n  Stripe's own checkout page.\n- Honest limit, without an account: the confirmation is cached\n  client-side, so someone with devtools could forge the cache for their own\n  browser. The check is real; the cache is not an enforcement boundary. See\n  docs/SECURITY.md.\n- With an account (email sign-in, below), the entitlement lives in D1,\n  written only by the Stripe webhook or a sales code, and the browser cache\n  is not consulted: every server scan, alert and digest checks the D1 record.\n\nA complete, always-current key list lives in code — search the source for the\nliteral key strings; the families above cover every `atlas.`- and `atlas:`-\nprefixed key the app writes.\n\n## What clears it\n\n| Action                                            | Clears                                                                                                                                                                                | Leaves behind (remanence)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Reset to demo data** (Settings → Demo Controls) | `atlas.byod.import.v1` (your imported data)                                                                                                                                           | All other UI-state keys remain — by design; reset restores the seeded catalog, it is not a privacy wipe                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Sign out**                                      | Every `atlas.`-prefixed key **except** `atlas.theme` (so a second user on a shared machine doesn't inherit the first user's snooze lists, saved views, session, or **imported data**) | `atlas.theme` (device preference) and **`atlas:`-colon-prefixed keys** (onboarding flags, command-palette recents, column presets, snooze state) — these are _not_ cleared by sign-out today. A **real account's** connected workspace is parked in `atlas:parked-workspaces.v1` under its email (tokens removed) so the same person signing back in keeps it; a different email starts empty |\n| **Browser \"clear site data\" / private window**    | Everything                                                                                                                                                                            | Nothing                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n\n### Known remanence (full disclosure)\n\n- **Reset to demo data clears only the import**, not other UI state. It is a\n  \"restore the demo catalog\" button, not a privacy wipe. To remove imported\n  data specifically, Reset is sufficient — the import lives in exactly one key.\n- **A real account's workspace survives sign-out, by design.** There is no\n  server to hold it, so sign-out parks it locally under the account's email\n  (`atlas:parked-workspaces.v1`, access tokens excluded) and the sign-in page\n  offers \"Welcome back\" to that email. On a shared machine, use \"Forget this\n  workspace\" on the sign-in page (or clear site data) to remove it.\n- **Sign-out clears `atlas.*` but not `atlas:*` keys.** The colon-namespaced\n  keys are non-sensitive UI flags (onboarding-seen, recent routes, column\n  presets, snooze state) and never hold imported data — the BYOD import is an\n  `atlas.`-key and _is_ cleared on sign-out. Still, for a guaranteed clean\n  slate on a shared machine, use the browser's \"clear site data\".\n\nIf either of these behaviors changes, update this table in the same PR (per\n[`docs/DOCUMENTATION_POLICY.md`](DOCUMENTATION_POLICY.md)).\n\n## Hosted-deploy product telemetry (opt-in at build time)\n\nEverything above describes the **default build** and remains true for it:\nno analytics beacon exists, and `VITE_POSTHOG_KEY` is **unset** for the\npublic demo deploy.\n\nA deployment that sets `VITE_POSTHOG_KEY` at build time (see\n[`ENV_VARS.md`](ENV_VARS.md)) opts into product analytics via PostHog, and\nthe honest consequences are:\n\n- **What loads.** `src/services/posthog.ts` injects the official posthog-js\n  bundle (`array.js`) from the configured PostHog host's assets origin —\n  Truehound bundles no analytics SDK of its own. The CSP is widened at build\n  time to exactly that host pair (`scripts/csp-plugin.mjs`), nothing else.\n  With no key, the loader injects nothing and makes zero network calls —\n  identical behavior to before this feature existed.\n- **What is sent.** Pageviews on every route change with the route path\n  **normalized** (`/incidents/INC-42` becomes `/incidents/:id` —\n  `src/services/routeNormalizer.ts`), autocaptured clicks with **all element\n  text masked** (`mask_all_text`), and named product events (signup, login,\n  integration connect outcomes, upgrade intent). Event props carry enums and\n  booleans, not arbitrary text.\n- **What is deliberately NOT sent.** Email addresses, never — `identify()`\n  forwards a role plus a distinct id, and an email-shaped id is replaced by\n  a non-reversible digest first (`src/services/analytics.ts`). No session\n  recording (`disable_session_recording`). Browser Do Not Track is honored\n  (`respect_dnt`). Imported/BYOD data and connected-source payloads never\n  pass through the analytics layer at all.\n- **What leaks anyway, stated plainly.** PostHog receives the visitor's IP\n  address and User-Agent as a property of any HTTP request, and\n  `$current_url` on events includes the full page URL (origin + hash\n  route). Clicked elements' tag/class/id attributes ride along with\n  autocapture. If that is unacceptable for your deployment, leave the key\n  unset — the local analytics buffer keeps working with no egress.\n- **The local buffer stays on.** Settings → recent events shows the same\n  trail whether or not PostHog is enabled; enabling PostHog adds a second\n  sink, it does not replace the first.\n\nVerification for telemetry-enabled builds lives in\n`scripts/uitest/verify-analytics.mjs` (see\n[`launch/TEST_MATRIX.md`](launch/TEST_MATRIX.md)): it intercepts every\nrequest bound for the PostHog ingestion host and asserts pageviews are\nnormalized before anything leaves the page.\n\n## Self-hosted fonts\n\nTruehound serves the Figtree and JetBrains Mono webfonts from its own origin\n(`public/fonts/*.woff2`) via same-origin `@font-face` declarations in\n`src/index.css`. Previously `src/index.css` did\n`@import url(\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/...\")`, which made the browser\nissue a request to Google Fonts on every page load — exposing the visitor's\n**IP address and User-Agent** to a third party before the app even rendered.\nThat is incompatible with \"your data never leaves this browser\", so it was\nremoved. The exact same `.woff2` files Google served are now shipped in the\nbundle; rendering is pixel-identical (variable fonts, latin + latin-ext\nsubsets with Google's canonical `unicode-range` split).\n\n## How this is verified\n\n- [`e2e/privacy.spec.ts`](../e2e/privacy.spec.ts) imports a CSV/JSON of\n  distinctive sentinel names, drives every data-rendering surface (including\n  `/catalog`'s DuckDB boot) and the command-palette search, and asserts:\n  (1) **no** network request — URL, query, POST body, or header — contains any\n  imported string; (2) **every** request is same-origin; (3) **no** known\n  telemetry/font/CDN host is contacted.\n- [`e2e/csp.spec.ts`](../e2e/csp.spec.ts) asserts the shipped CSP allowlists\n  **no** third-party origin and that a full walkthrough fires zero\n  `securitypolicyviolation` events.\n\nBoth are Playwright suites: they run with `npm run e2e` (locally and before a\nrelease), not in `npm run ci`. `src/pages/legal/privacyClaims.test.tsx` does run\nin `npm run ci`: it reads the retention periods, cookie flags, encryption,\nmailbox scopes, extension payload and analytics settings back from the code\nand fails if the public Privacy Policy, DPA or sub-processor list disagrees.\n\n## Access Audit (added 2026-10-20)\n\n- **The scan runs in the browser.** `src/data/access/githubAccess.ts` calls `api.github.com` directly with the customer's token, with one exception below. The token is kept for this browser session only (`sessionStorage`, `src/data/live/tokenStore.ts`) unless the customer ticks \"Remember this token on this device\" (then `localStorage`). \"Forget token\" on the audit page removes it everywhere; fixes and re-scans then ask for it again. Always watching re-scans on visit only when the token is remembered (or still in this session).\n- **GitHub search goes through the connector relay (added 2026-10-21).** GitHub answers code search (`GET /search/code`) without a CORS header, so no browser can read it, and commit search (`GET /search/commits`) fails at the network level in Chromium. When the connector's `/health` lists `github-search`, the scan sends those two calls to `GET <connector>/github/search/code` and `/github/search/commits` instead (`connector/github-search.mjs`). What passes through: the search query (`q`, which holds the org name, a login or a secret-rule search term such as `\"AKIA\"`, plus `per_page`, `page`, `sort`, `order`), the customer's token in the `Authorization` header, and GitHub's answer (file paths, repo names, commit authors and dates, and GitHub's paging and rate-limit headers). The relay forwards only to `https://api.github.com/search/code` or `/search/commits` (a fixed host and path, never a client-supplied URL), sends `Cache-Control: no-store`, and never stores, caches, logs or echoes the token or the results. File contents are still fetched directly from `api.github.com` by the browser, never through the relay. Without the relay, the CODEOWNERS and secrets checks say \"GitHub doesn't let browsers run code search, so this couldn't be checked here.\" and last activity is marked partial.\n- **Secrets are never stored or sent.** File contents fetched for the secret-pattern check are scanned in memory and dropped; only the repo, path, line, rule and a masked preview (`AKIA************WXYZ`) are kept (`src/data/access/secretPatterns.ts`). Webhook URLs are reduced to their host, because a URL can carry a token.\n- **What is kept, and where.** `atlas.access.real.v1` (real accounts) holds the last snapshot (people, roles, 2FA status, grants, deploy-key metadata, webhook hosts, public repos, masked secret previews, CODEOWNERS user entries), the departed list the customer pasted or uploaded, manual marks, \"still here\" confirmations and known webhook hosts. `atlas.access.demo.v1` holds the demo visitor's edits to the fictional Acme audit. Both keys start with `atlas.access`, so reset and sign-out clear them.\n- **Departed status is the customer's.** Nobody is called departed unless they are on the customer's list or marked by hand.\n- **One-click fixes** (remove member, demote owner, remove outside collaborator, delete deploy key) are GitHub API calls from the browser with the customer's token, each after an explicit confirmation; each attempt is sealed in the browser's audit chain.\n- **AI (optional).** Only when AI is enabled on the connector and the customer clicks \"Draft with AI\": `/ai/access-plan` receives the score, the counts, and finding titles and places (logins, repo names, file paths). Never the token, never a secret value.\n- **Free scan (`/scan`, added 2026-10-21).** A visitor with no account runs the same in-browser scan. The token goes to GitHub (and through the connector for GitHub search only) and is not saved. The result is kept in this browser under `atlas.access.freeScan.v1` (org metadata, the visitor's own \"Who left?\" text, no token, secrets masked) and shown with every name, repo and fix locked. If the visitor signs up in this browser, it becomes that account's audit (`atlas.access.real.v1`) and the free-scan key is removed. Nothing is sent to us.\n- **Ask Truehound (optional AI investigator, added 2026-10-21).** Off unless the workspace has added its own Anthropic key in Settings > AI (it then shows \"Not set up\"). Even with a key, nothing is sent until the customer clicks \"Agree and turn on Ask Truehound\" for that org; the consent is kept per org in `atlas.access.aiConsent.v1` (cleared by reset and sign-out) and withdrawn with \"Stop sending this scan to AI\". Then, only on a click (Investigate, Plan it with AI, Ask), the browser posts the scan as numbered facts built by `src/data/access/askTruehound.ts` to `/ai/access-investigate`, `/ai/access-cleanup` or `/ai/access-question`: logins, the names and roles GitHub shares, 2FA status, last activity seen, repo access, team and CODEOWNERS entries, deploy key titles and dates, webhook hosts, public repo names, secret-finding locations (rule, repo, file, line; never the value and not even the masked preview), the findings, the checks that could not run, and the question. The connector forwards them to Anthropic (Claude) and stores neither request nor answer. Never the GitHub token. Results are labelled \"AI-written, check before acting.\"; the AI has no tools and cannot change GitHub.\n- **The report** (Markdown or print to PDF) and the share card are generated in the browser. The share card shows only the score.\n\n## Sources beyond GitHub (added 2026-10-21, behind VITE_SOURCES)\n\n- **Google Workspace is read from the browser.** Sign-in is Google's own window (Google Identity Services). The access token stays in the tab's memory for about an hour and is never saved, logged or sent anywhere but Google (`admin.googleapis.com`, `www.googleapis.com`). Read-only scopes first; the write scopes (suspend a user, remove an app's access) are asked only when the customer ticks Allow fixes.\n- **Slack is read from the browser.** The token the customer pastes is sent only to `slack.com`, as a form field in a POST body (Slack's CORS allows no Authorization header), never in a URL.\n- **Microsoft Entra ID is read from the browser.** Sign-in is Microsoft's own window (authorization code with PKCE, no client secret, no refresh token). The access token stays in the tab's memory for about an hour and is never saved, logged or sent anywhere but Microsoft (`graph.microsoft.com`, `login.microsoftonline.com`). Read-only permissions first; turning off sign-in and ending sessions are asked only when the customer ticks Allow fixes.\n- **Rippling is read from the browser.** The token the customer pastes is sent only to `rest.ripplingapis.com`, in the Authorization header, never in a URL. Truehound reads worker status, end dates, names and emails; it never reads pay, tax or bank fields.\n- **Pipedrive is read from the browser.** The token is sent only to `api.pipedrive.com`, in the `x-api-token` header, never in a URL.\n- **Work apps read from the browser** (Atlassian, Linear, Notion, Asana, Trello, Sentry, Miro, Dropbox): the pasted key or token is sent only to that vendor's API (`api.atlassian.com`, `api.linear.app`, `api.notion.com`, `app.asana.com`, `api.trello.com`, `sentry.io`, `api.miro.com`, `api.dropboxapi.com`) in its documented header, never in a URL.\n- **Relay sources** (PagerDuty, JumpCloud, Deel, HubSpot, Intercom, Brex, Ramp, Mercury, Salesforce, Datadog, Opsgenie, New Relic, monday.com, Okta, BambooHR, ClickUp, Figma, Zoom, Box, Grafana Cloud, HiBob, Personio, Gusto, Workday, Kombo) go through the connector's generic relay, `/relay/<app>/...` (`connector/relay.mjs`): one allow-listed vendor host per app (for Salesforce, the customer's own `<my-domain>.my.salesforce.com`, checked against a pattern), the token forwarded per request and never stored, cached or logged. For Zoom (Server-to-Server OAuth) and Box (Client Credentials Grant), the relay trades the pasted app credentials for a short-lived access token at the vendor's own token endpoint (`zoom.us/oauth/token`, `api.box.com/oauth2/token`) on each request; neither the credentials nor the token are kept. Personio's client ID and secret are traded the same way at `api.personio.de/v2/auth/token`. From HR systems Truehound keeps names, emails, title, department, manager, location, status and start and termination dates only; any pay or bank field in a vendor's response is dropped in the browser and never stored.\n- **Apps on a per-customer host** (Salesforce, Zendesk, Freshdesk, Okta, BambooHR, Grafana Cloud, Figma, Workday): the host part the customer types (`acme` for acme.zendesk.com; for Figma, its SCIM Tenant ID) is kept in `atlas.sources.tenant.<app>.v1`. It is not a secret.\n- **Cloudflare, Supabase, Heroku, Docker Hub and npm go through the same relay** (they do not answer browsers, or need headers a browser cannot send). One host each: `api.cloudflare.com`, `api.supabase.com`, `api.heroku.com`, `hub.docker.com`, `registry.npmjs.org`. The pasted token is forwarded per request and never stored, cached or logged; for Docker Hub the connector first trades it for Docker's 10-minute token, which is also never kept.\n- **SCIM apps go through the same relay** (added 2026-10-22): one entry per app in the SCIM catalog (`connector/scim/registry.mjs`), each limited to that app's documented host and SCIM `/Users` path, the SCIM token forwarded per request and never stored, cached or logged.\n- **Connectors a customer builds** go through the account API's custom relay (`connector/api/v1/custom.mjs`), only to https hosts an admin of that workspace allowed, on public addresses. The definition (URLs, field mapping) is stored with the workspace; the app's credential is forwarded per request and never stored.\n- **Screenshots and PDFs of a user list** (manual apps, assisted removal) are sent to the connector's `POST /ai/extract-access`, read by Claude (Anthropic) and not kept by Truehound; only the rows a person confirms, and the file's SHA-256, are kept in this browser and in Proof. Without `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` on the connector nothing is sent.\n- **REST catalog apps go through the same relay** (added 2026-10-22): one entry per app (`connector/rest/registry.mjs`), each limited to that app's documented host, users path and removal call; the app's API token is forwarded per request and never stored, cached or logged.\n- **Browser playbooks** (accounts only, opt-in, added 2026-10-22): for an app with no user API on the customer's plan, an owner or admin may store a service account's sign-in (email, password, optional authenticator secret) in `playbook_connections`, **AES-GCM encrypted** with a key derived from `PLATFORM_SECRET`, never returned to a browser and removed by Disconnect. A run signs in through Cloudflare Browser Rendering, may only visit that app's own hosts, and keeps in `playbook_runs` what it did (list, verify or remove, the target's email), whether it held, who started it and, for a removal, the screenshot taken after the re-read and its SHA-256 (also sealed in Proof); the member list it reads goes to the browser that asked and is not kept on the server. Proposed selector repairs (`playbook_proposals`) hold page selectors only.\n- **The public API and webhooks** (accounts only): what an admin's browser publishes for `/api/v1` (people, accounts, systems, findings, leavers, reviews, offboardings, evidence manifests) is stored with the workspace, credential-like fields dropped; API keys are stored only as SHA-256; webhook signing secrets are stored encrypted (AES-GCM).\n- **AWS IAM goes through the connector, with no customer key.** The customer pastes a role ARN and an external ID (kept like any pasted token). For each request the connector assumes that read-only role in the customer's account with Truehound's own AWS key (a Worker secret), signs the calls to `sts.amazonaws.com`, `iam.amazonaws.com` and, when asked, the Identity Center endpoints of one region, and answers the results. The short-lived session credentials are never stored, cached, logged or returned to the browser. Deleting the customer's CloudFormation stack removes Truehound's access.\n- **Apps reviewed by hand** (Stripe's team list, Mailchimp, QuickBooks Online, Xero, and any app added by hand): the CSV or pasted table is read in the browser and never sent anywhere. The people it lists are kept like any scan, in `atlas.sources.real.v1`, with the file's name and SHA-256; the names of apps added by hand are kept in `atlas.sources.manualApps.v1`.\n- **What is kept, and where.** `atlas.sources.real.v1` holds the last scan of each app (people, roles, status, second factor, last activity, app grants), the customer's match decisions, their access reviews and offboarding runs. `atlas.sources.demo.v1` holds the demo visitor's edits. Pasted tokens are kept in `atlas.sources.token.<app>.v1` and removed by Disconnect. Reset and sign-out clear all of them, like every other key above.\n- **The optional AI match helper** sends one unclear account and its candidates (names, handles, emails, roles, status, the deterministic evidence) to the connector's existing `/ai/access-question`, only after the same per-org consent as Ask Truehound. Never a token.\n- **The evidence pack** (CSV files, manifest with SHA-256 hashes, ZIP) is built in the browser.\n\n## Accounts (server-side, added 2026-10-20)\n\nAn account is optional. A person who signs in with their email gets a\nworkspace kept in Cloudflare D1 by the connector Worker (`connector/api/`,\nschema in `connector/migrations/`), so a team can share it, a second admin\ncan approve from their own device, and the GitHub App can watch the org.\ndocs/BACKEND.md is the operator's guide.\n\n**Stored, per table:**\n\n| Table                                        | What                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Kept                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `users`                                      | email, optional name                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | until the account is deleted                                                  |\n| `orgs`, `memberships`                        | workspace name, GitHub org login, each member's role                                                                                                                                                                                      | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `invites`                                    | invited email, role, sha256 of the invite token                                                                                                                                                                                           | until accepted, revoked or the workspace is deleted                           |\n| `magic_links`                                | sha256 of the link token, email, sha256 of the requesting IP, landing path                                                                                                                                                                | 24 hours                                                                      |\n| `sessions`                                   | sha256 of the session token, CSRF token                                                                                                                                                                                                   | 30 days from the last visit                                                   |\n| `workspaces`                                 | departed list, marks, still-here, known hosts, the last scan snapshot (masked secrets only), the approval policy                                                                                                                          | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `audit_events`                               | the sealed audit chain (the browser's history is imported once, verified)                                                                                                                                                                 | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `fix_requests`, `fix_approvals`              | requested fixes, who approved, overrides with reasons                                                                                                                                                                                     | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `github_installations`                       | installation id, org login, granted permissions                                                                                                                                                                                           | until the workspace is deleted (unlinked, not deleted, on unlink)             |\n| `github_install_states`, `github_deliveries` | install-link state hashes; webhook delivery ids                                                                                                                                                                                           | 30 minutes; 7 days                                                            |\n| `scans`                                      | each server scan's findings, score, counts, what was new and fixed                                                                                                                                                                        | 90 days, the latest always                                                    |\n| `alert_channels`, `alert_log`                | member email or Slack webhook URL, severity, unsubscribe token; what was sent                                                                                                                                                             | until removed; 90 days                                                        |\n| `entitlements`, `stripe_events`              | plan, paying email, Stripe session, customer and subscription ids; event ids                                                                                                                                                              | until the workspace is deleted (billing records also stay in Stripe); 30 days |\n| `req_apps`, `req_bundles`, `req_settings`    | the request catalog (app, owners, roles, who approves, the provisioner's non-secret settings such as a SCIM base URL), role-based bundles, task and ticket settings, sha256 of the HR webhook and SCIM tokens                             | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `req_people`                                 | the lifecycle directory from HR events, SCIM and the form: email, name, manager's email, department, title, active or left with dates, GitHub login                                                                                       | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `req_requests`, `req_approvals`              | access requests: app, role, the person, the reason, how long, each approver's decision and reason, what the app's API answered and the re-read                                                                                            | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `req_runs`, `req_run_approvals`, `req_steps` | joiner, mover and offboarding runs and their steps (hand-over details: repo names, Drive transfer id, PagerDuty schedule ids, domains)                                                                                                    | until the workspace is deleted                                                |\n| `req_tasks`, `req_links`                     | owner tasks (title, steps, assignee email, due date, reminders, the evidence note and link, the ticket key and status); sha256 of approval and completion links                                                                           | until the workspace is deleted; links 30 days after they expire               |\n| `req_secrets`                                | credentials an admin saves for grants, removals, hand-overs, Slack and tickets, **AES-GCM encrypted** with a key derived per workspace from the `LIFECYCLE_SECRET` Worker secret; only a hint (last 4 characters, the site) is ever shown | until removed on Setup or the workspace is deleted                            |\n| `req_slack_teams`, `req_slack_states`        | the Slack workspace id and name connected to a workspace; install-link state hashes                                                                                                                                                       | until disconnected; 30 minutes                                                |\n\nRetention runs in the 15-minute Cron Trigger (`connector/api/jobs.mjs`).\n\n**Never stored:** a pasted GitHub token (it stays in the browser), the\nGitHub App's installation tokens (in memory, one hour at most), file\ncontents or a secret's value (only masked previews), card numbers.\n\n**In this browser, for an account:** `atlas.account.org.v1` (which\nworkspace this browser works in) and `atlas.account.imported.v1` (the\nworkspaces the browser-only workspace was already imported into). The CSRF\ntoken stays in memory. The browser's entitlement cache is not read for a\nsigned-in account.\n\n**One cookie:** `__Host-th_session` (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax) on the\nAPI host, set only on sign-in. The SPA keeps the CSRF token in memory.\n\n**Deletion:** an owner deletes a workspace (`DELETE /api/orgs/:id`, every\nrow above cascades), a person deletes their account (`DELETE /api/me`, with\nevery workspace where they are the only member). Uninstalling the GitHub App\non GitHub stops every read; the `installation.deleted` webhook marks it.\n\n**Outbound, only with an account:** Resend receives the recipient and the\nmessage for sign-in links, invites, alerts and the digest; Slack receives\nalerts an admin pointed at a webhook; GitHub receives the App's reads (and\nthe Fixes app's writes, after approval).\n\n**Access requests and the lifecycle (added 2026-10-22), only when an admin\nconnects them:** the Slack Web API (`slack.com`) receives DMs to approvers,\nrequesters and app owners, and Truehound looks people up there by email;\nan app's own API receives the grants, removals and re-reads (SCIM 2.0 for\nFigma, Slack, Atlassian, Notion and Zoom; `api.github.com`;\n`admin.googleapis.com` for group membership and the Drive Data Transfer;\n`api.pagerduty.com`); Jira Cloud (`<site>.atlassian.net`) or ServiceNow\n(`<instance>.service-now.com`) receive a ticket per manual step. The stored\ncredential is decrypted in the Worker only for that call and never logged\nor returned. An identity provider pushing to Truehound's SCIM server, and\nan HR system calling the webhook, send people's work details (email, name,\ndepartment, title, manager, status).\n\n\n## Discovery: mailbox metadata and the browser extension (added 2026-10-22)\n\nBehind `VITE_SOURCES` (off on truehound.io).\n\n- **Mailbox metadata** (`src/data/discovery/mail.ts`): opt-in per mailbox, with a consent tick. Scopes `gmail.metadata` and `Mail.ReadBasic` only. Reads From, Subject and Date of up to 1,500 newest messages in the browser; keeps only sign-up and invoice mail as sender domain, subject (140 characters), date and mailbox. The OAuth token stays in tab memory. \"Withdraw and delete what was read\" removes it; consent and withdrawal go to the Proof log.\n- **Card and bank spend** (`src/data/discovery/spend.ts`): Brex, Ramp and Mercury transactions through the relay; only charges matching a software vendor are kept (vendor, amount, date, statement line).\n- **Browser extension** (`extension/`): records registered domain, time (to the minute), `via` (password or sso) and the work email if set; sends `{ user, sightings }` to `POST /api/discovery/sightings` every 30 minutes when configured. The connector stores domain, at, user_email, via, key_id and received_at in `discovery_sightings`, deleted 395 days (13 months) after the sign-in by the retention job (`connector/api/jobs.mjs`).\n\n## Retention changes (2026-10-22)\n\n- Leads (`connector/leads.mjs`): each lead is written with a 24-month `expirationTtl`. Unsubscribe records have none.\n- Extension sightings: deleted after 395 days.\n- IP addresses: kept in memory for rate limits, or stored only as SHA-256 hashes (sign-in links, review links, auditor links; the auditor access log also keeps the user agent).\n";export{e as default};